The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
retval = tty_ldisc_lock(tty, 5 * HZ);
if (!tty->ldisc)
    retval = tty_ldisc_reinit(tty, tty->termios.c_line);
```
Reason:
Holding the line discipline lock during the `tty_reopen()` process can block ongoing I/O operations on the tty. This is particularly problematic when a user is entering a long password during a login process, as the lock prevents the I/O from completing, leading to user-visible issues.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ld = tty_ldisc_ref_wait(tty);
if (ld) {
    tty_ldisc_deref(ld);
} else {
    retval = tty_ldisc_lock(tty, 5 * HZ);
    if (!tty->ldisc)
        retval = tty_ldisc_reinit(tty, tty->termios.c_line);
}
```
Reason:
The patch optimizes the `tty_reopen()` function by first checking if a line discipline is present without locking the write side. If a line discipline is present, it avoids unnecessary locking and reinitialization, thus preventing interruptions to ongoing I/O operations. This change ensures that the login process is not disrupted, especially when a user is entering a long password.

